{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>What shall we say to my lord.</b> Meaning: speech in the manner of appeasement. <b>What shall we speak.</b> Meaning, difficult speech in the manner of battle. <b>How shall we justify ourselves.</b> Meaning, in judgment if we come before judges, for there is no escape for them in any of them, and this is nothing but the deed of Elohim — He [uses] them to exact punishment from them for their sin when He finds an opportunity to collect His debt. And since the sin in their hands belongs to all of them together: ״הִנֶּנּוּ עֲבָדִים לַאדֹנִי״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it intends in his saying <b>how shall we justify ourselves</b>, that from now on, since the goblet was found in Benjamin's sack, there is no longer room for the kal va-chomer stated in their words ״הֵן כֶּסֶף״ etc., for Benjamin was not among those who returned the money." "<b>Also we.</b> Meaning, even though the theft was not found in our hands. And his saying <b>also with whom</b> etc., meaning, since the sin that was acknowledged among them does not include Benjamin in the sin, therefore they said ״גַּם אֲשֶׁר נִמְצָא״ etc., in the manner of their statement (Sanhedrin 93b) — two dry logs and one wet, etc. And since all of them were caught in their sin and he was with them, he was caught in their corruption." "<b>Or</b> it may intend that one should not understand that they were still in their initial position when they said ״אֲשֶׁר יִמָּצֵא אִתּוֹ וָמֵת וְגַם אֲנַחְנוּ״ — therefore they concluded by saying that there is no agreement to death among them, but rather גַּם אֲשֶׁר נִמְצָא — the judgment of slavery applies to him and not the judgment of death. And for the reason that there are no witnesses to the theft, and he could say ״לֹא יָדַעְתִּי מִי שָׂם״ etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 44, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:48:35.288947552Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}